  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 310 AM EDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nThe long term will bring continued persistent dry weather with \ncooler temperatures moderating through next week. By Sunday, surface \nhigh pressure centered from New England into the Northeast will \nextend southwestward into the region. Meanwhile, midlevel ridging \nwill begin to extend its grip eastward. Cool, breezy northeast winds \nwill be enhanced by the pressure gradient between the aforementioned \nhigh and a developing low in the western Atlantic. Combined with \ntemperatures remaining below normal (highs only reaching the low to \nmid 60s for most), Sunday will feel decidedly Fall-like.\n\nThough morning lows will remain in the 40s to low 50s through the \nweek, daytime highs will begin a more noticeable warming trend by \nMonday. Highs will begin to nudge back toward the 70s for most areas \nby Monday with widespread 70s thereafter. All-in-all, next week will \nbring pleasant and seasonal conditions. \n\nAs far as rainfall prospects are concerned, continued high pressure \nat the surface and ridging aloft will keep things bone dry. In fact, \nensembles continue to indicate likely dry weather extending toward \nthe end of October.",
